  {
    "episode_id": 95371,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95371.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, it failed so far, we're still having the discussions. The way I frame it now is that we have two valid interests, right? So you have people that in many cases have lived in their suburban style neighborhood for 30 years, and there's that meadow across the street, they always thought it might become a housing track or develop some point. But suddenly, it's going to be an oil rig. It's an industrial process right across the street from their home, maybe 500 feet or 550 feet or 600 feet from their actual house. And I think that has alarmed many people. So I think they have a legitimate right to be concerned. They purchase this house. They've lived there in quiet enjoyment. But on the other side, you've got people that have owned the mineral rights. And out here in Colorado, the person who owns the rights to harvest those minerals from under the ground generally isn't the same person who owns the surface rights. I mean, you don't want to have a situation where government's coming in and suddenly saying what you used to have that was open worse worth $10,000 is now only worth $3,000. So what we're trying to figure out is how to balance and so the both sides give a little. What is that? Are there ways that the oil and gas industry can dramatically lower the noise, the visual imposition, the sense that it's not the old neighborhood that they knew. How can we do that? Can we put some of this stuff underground, get more of the trucks off the roads, have much, much quieter activities in every sense, triple the fines for leaks if you're close to these neighborhoods. All kinds of things that we would figure out some way that people can still, people who own the mineral rights can still have the value, their asset, you know, that hasn't been dramatically diminished, but at the same time allow people living on the surface to have quiet enjoyment. It's a",
