    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95371.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "You know, I try not to look at it politically, right? And I may be running for reelection, but pretty likely I'm going to I'm going to really piss off both sides the way we're going. So I think it's better not to worry about that, but to look at what's fair, right? To a large extent, this is a land use issue. I think more than an issue around fracking and pollution. We're fracking 4,500 wells a year. And out here in Colorado, when we do hydraulic fracturing, it's usually one and a half to two miles below where the groundwater is. I mean, it just never comes up into the groundwater. I think what alarms people far more is this notion of having an industrial activity in their neighborhood. And I think there, we can perhaps find a compromise and say, all right, let's sit down and figure out what are the things we all agree to that people's property is something that should be protected. We shouldn't just have government take away the value of someone's property, but we should also recognize that somebody's, you know, right to be able to enjoy their house and their yard and maybe not have the intrusion quite so close of industrial activity that that has value. So how do we balance those two core values that I think most of us respect both of them? And that's the magic we haven't been able to find yet, is where is those compromises so that a neighborhood doesn't feel they're losing their quality of life. But at the same time, someone who owns a mineral right is able to get the value that they've always counted on. Many of these people that own the mineral rights are retired. And this is part of their retirement. Colorado Governor John Hickenlooper, sir, thank you very much for your time. It was my pleasure.",
